      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by converting the logarithmic expressions to their exponential forms.\n\nFirst, consider the right-hand side of the equation:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3\n\\]\nThis means that \\(3\\) is the number such that when raised to the power of \\(\\log_{27} 3\\), the result is \\(27\\). In other words,\n\\[\n27 = 3^{\\log_{27} 3}\n\\]\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can substitute to get:\n\\[\n3^3 = 3^{\\log_{27} 3}\n\\]\nBy equating the exponents, we find:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = 3\n\\]\nSo, the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = 3\\) means:\n\\[\nx^3 = 4\n\\]\nTo find \\(x\\), we take the cube root of both sides:\n\\[\nx = \\sqrt[3]{4}\n\\]\nThus, the solution to the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt[3]{4}}\n\\]",
