      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing both logarithms with a common base. We use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\).\n\nFirst, we express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in terms of base 10 or base \\(e\\). Using the change of base formula with base 10, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can rewrite \\(\\log_{10} 27\\) as \\(\\log_{10} (3^3) = 3 \\log_{10} 3\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{3 \\log_{10} 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nSo, the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis means that \\(x\\) satisfies \\(x^{1/3} = 4\\), or equivalently,\n\\[\nx = 4^3 = 64\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
