      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to express both logarithms in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms (ln), but let's use the change of base formula to simplify our work. The change of base formula states:\n\\[\n\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\n\\]\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) and \\(\\log_x 4\\) in terms of base 10 logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal, we set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert the logarithms of the bases \\(27\\) and \\(x\\) to base 10:\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 27 = \\log_{10} (3^3) = 3 \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10} x = \\log_{10} x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 4 = \\log_{10} (2^2) = 2 \\log_{10} 2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute these into the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{3 \\log_{10} 3}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log_{10} 2}{3 \\log_{10} 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\(\\log_{10} 2\\) by isolating it:\n\\[\n2 \\log_{10} 2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3 \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_{10} 2 = \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(x\\), we use the fact that the logarithm base \\(x\\) of 4 must equal \\(\\log_{27} 3\\):\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nRewriting this in exponential form:\n\\[\n4 = x^{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]\n\nCubing both sides to solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = 4^3\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
