      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\( x \\) such that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms.**\n   \\[\n   \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have \\(\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\\). Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   So, the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes \\(\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\n2. **Rewrite the logarithmic equation in exponential form.**\n   \\[\n   \\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies x^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4\n   \\]\n   To solve for \\( x \\), we raise both sides of the equation to the power of 3:\n   \\[\n   (x^{\\frac{1}{3}})^3 = 4^3 \\implies x = 64\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
